WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.550
2021's "Mortal Kombat" may be a faithful video
game movie, but it still makes some major

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.710
changes to the games' classic kombatants.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.380
Here are all the major differences between
the characters in the games and the movie.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.540
Sub-Zero has become a standard-bearer of the
"Mortal Kombat" series, to the point that

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.470
the phrase "Mortal Kombat" likely conjures
his enigmatically masked face in many fans'

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:21.470
minds.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.589
He's appeared as a playable fighter in every
single new "Mortal Kombat" game since the

00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:27.499
very first one arrived in arcades in 1992.

00:00:27.499 --> 00:00:32.509
So it makes sense that Sub-Zero freeze-blasts
his way on to the silver screen as the 2021

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:33.760
movie's primary villain.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.500
But there's a little detail about this cinematic
Sub-Zero most viewers aren't going to pick

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.520
up on, which reveals him to be a different
Sub-Zero than the one featured in the modern

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.950
slate of "Mortal Kombat" games.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.890
"Bi-Han!"

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.330
Sub-Zero begins the movie under his given
name, Bi-Han.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:56.930
But Bi-Han is not the Sub-Zero most "Mortal
Kombat" players know and love, unless their

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.820
frame of reference is firmly rooted in the
early '90s.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.239
Indeed, Bi-Han is the original Sub-Zero, featured
in the very first "Mortal Kombat" game.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.679
But in that inaugural game, Scorpion kills
Bi-Han.

00:01:07.679 --> 00:01:12.210
In subsequent entries, his younger brother,
Kuai Liang, takes up the Sub-Zero mantle.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.409
It is Kuai Liang, rather than Bi-Han, who
appears as Sub-Zero in pretty much every game

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.479
since 1993's "Mortal Kombat II."

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:22.790
The exception to this occurs in the 2011 "Mortal
Kombat" game, in which both versions of the

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.060
character are featured.

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:29.500
Scorpion's origins are humble: His first design
is exactly the same as Sub-Zero's, save for

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.969
his yellow color scheme.

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:34.770
Since those early days, however, Scorpion
has gone on to become one of the most iconic

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.270
"Mortal Kombat" characters around, second
only to his icy counterpart.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.979
He goes by his birth name, Hanzo Hasashi,
for most of the 2021 movie, and isn't featured

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:45.200
as heavily as many fans might have been expecting.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.810
In fact, he dies in the prologue, and doesn't
reappear until he gets his revenge on Sub-Zero

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.249
in the final minutes of the film.

00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:54.499
"Get over here!"

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:58.840
There are two key differences between Scorpion
in the new "Mortal Kombat" movie and Scorpion

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.380
in the "Mortal Kombat" games.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.289
In the first game, as in the movie, Scorpion
is driven to hunt down Sub-Zero after the

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:07.520
cryomancer kills his family ... or so he believes.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.470
According to the games' lore, it is actually
Netherrealm demon Quan Chi who commits the

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.420
atrocious killing — but Scorpion doesn't
learn this until multiple entries later.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.220
Related to this point is the second key difference
between the cinematic Scorpion and his game-bound

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.220
counterpart.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.630
The movie introduces a major new twist to
his origin: A surviving heir.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.170
In 2021's "Mortal Kombat," Hanzo's youngest
daughter manages to survive the massacre,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.640
and goes on to continue a bloodline that eventually
results in the birth of Cole.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.390
Aside from newbie Cole and his family, Sonya
Blade is the only classic "Mortal Kombat"

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.530
character to get a brand new character arc
in the 2021 film.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.940
This movie brings a new facet to the complex
lore of "Mortal Kombat:" The dragon mark.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.420
The dragon mark is a small brand in the shape
of the "Mortal Kombat" logo, and is what designates

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.330
fighters as kombatants and gives them their
arcana-based powers.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.070
Cole is born with his dragon mark, but many
of the movie's fighters earn theirs in the

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.090
only other way a person might hope to do so:
They kill someone who already has the mark.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.760
When we meet Sonya early on, she is unmarked.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.290
Any "Mortal Kombat" fan could tell you that
it's not a group of Earthrealm protectors

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:12.330
without Sonya Blade, so it's not a surprise
that this state of affairs doesn't last.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.180
This is the core of Sonya's story in "Mortal
Kombat:" Her journey ends when she takes the

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:20.570
dragon mark from Kano by killing him with
a pretty gnarly stab through the head.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:24.980
He's a suitable victim for Sonya, given their
long-running rivalry in the "Mortal Kombat"

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.980
games and comics.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:31.230
Kano, as depicted in the 2021 movie, is a
foul-mouthed mercenary who feels very much

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.750
in keeping with the spirit of the game character
... with one notable exception.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.820
The vast majority of the "Mortal Kombat" games'
storytelling positions Kano as the nemesis

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.880
of Sonya Blade and Jax.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.920
So it's a bit of a twist to see him helping
Liu Kang train Cole in the 2021 film.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:45.920
Or is it?

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.930
We hate to ruin the fun and suspense, but
any "Mortal Kombat" fan worth their salt can

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.440
tell you that Kano is the master of betrayal.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.600
Without completely giving away the details
of the movie, let's just say that "Mortal

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.380
Kombat" delivers on Kano and Sonya Blade's
rivalry, and makes Kano the lovable scumbag

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:01.620
he truly is.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.930
"You f---in' beauty."

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.900
Another interesting difference between Kano
of the movie and Kano of the games lies in

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.011
his status within the Black Dragon clan: In
the current timeline of the games, Kano is

00:04:11.011 --> 00:04:14.070
the group's leader, but in the new film, he
is merely a member.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:18.400
The only other thing missing is his cybernetic
heart, which allows him to shoot out blasts

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.549
from his chest to annihilate his foes.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:23.920
Hopefully, we'll be seeing a little of that
action in subsequent "Mortal Kombat" movies.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.110
Of all the main characters featured in 2021's
"Mortal Kombat," Jax is the only fighter who

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.620
isn't part of the first game's roster.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.300
Introduced in "Mortal Kombat II," special
forces agent Jax Briggs instantly immortalized

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.030
himself, and has appeared as a playable character
in almost every "Mortal Kombat" game since.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.730
Unless you haven't touched the series since
his introduction in "MK II," you probably

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:49.270
noticed something off about Jax at the start
of the movie ... or, to be specific, two things.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.500
That's right: In the movie, Jax has normal
human arms!

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.040
Ever since "Mortal Kombat III," Jax has been
known best for his titanium, cybernetic arms,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.460
which allow him to go toe-to-toe with dark
sorcerers and outright gods.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.480
This might seem like a shocking departure
few fans would be able to embrace, let alone

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.760
get excited about, but consider this: Now
we get to see this transformation happen,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.340
in all its gory glory.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:15.270
In this rendition of the "Mortal Kombat" story,
it is Sub-Zero who brutally freezes and dismembers

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.270
Jax.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:20.570
He gets his shiny synthetic limbs just in
time to kick plenty of Outworld butt and absolutely

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.040
destroy Reiko with his iconic "head clap"
fatality.

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:29.860
A warrior monk and champion of Earthrealm,
Liu Kang usually acts as the protagonist of

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.710
the "Mortal Kombat" game series.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.890
This was directly translated to film in the
original 1995 "Mortal Kombat" movie.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:41.230
The 2021 film, in contrast, introduces Cole
Young as the new main character, pushing Liu

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:42.830
Kang into a mentor role.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.110
This is a serious departure from the much
larger role Liu Kang typically plays in "Mortal

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.200
Kombat" stories, which is made even more dramatic
by the fact that this "chosen one" takes a

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.940
backseat to many other major players in the
film.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.720
Liu Kang's design in the 2021 movie is inspired
by his look in the early "Mortal Kombat" games,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.540
as is the case with many of the film's other
fighters.

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:05.880
Noticeably absent from his cinematic arsenal
are the nunchaku he uses as a weapon in the

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.100
PlayStation 2 trilogy of 3D "Mortal Kombat"
games.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:13.460
Those nunchaku were recently brought back
as Kang's primary weapon in 2019's "Mortal

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.760
Kombat 11."

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.270
Anyone who has only checked out that game
will find Liu Kang's silver screen fighting

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.580
style to be quite different from what they've
come to expect.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.580
Slicing through his opponents with his unforgettable
wide-brimmed hat, Shaolin monk Kung Lao does

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.740
a whole lot of killing and not much talking
in 2021's "Mortal Kombat".

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.570
That's far from a complaint: We're all here
for the action, after all.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:37.500
The new movie thankfully ticks most of the
boxes when it comes to this fan-favorite character.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.970
Of course, his iconic hat, famously inspired
by alpha-henchman Oddjob's topper in 1964's

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:47.570
"Goldfinger," is a bloody tour-de-force, but
Kung Lao also does plenty of teleporting throughout

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:48.870
the movie's runtime.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:53.199
Mentioned too is his lineage: He is a descendant
of the Great Kung Lao, legendary champion

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:54.199
of Earthrealm.

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:59.010
The 2021 film also adds another new wrinkle
to the mythology of "Mortal Kombat:" Here,

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.800
Liu Kang and Kung Lao are cousins.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.940
Antagonist Shang Tsung doesn't take up much
screen time at all in the 2021 film, despite

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.050
the fact that he's Liu Kang's rival and the
main villain of the first "Mortal Kombat"

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.050
game.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.661
In this movie, the rivalry between Shang Tsung
and Liu Kang isn't emphasized, though there

00:07:15.661 --> 00:07:19.200
is some groundwork laid for it in a potential
future movie.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.190
Absent too is any mention of Tsung's origin
as a Chinese human born in Earthrealm, who

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:27.150
was given his powers over dark sorcery by
Outworld emperor Shao Kahn.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.120
That's not where the differences end, either.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.080
Aesthetically, the Shang Tsung we see in the
movie doesn't resemble any of the Shang Tsungs

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.900
we've seen in the "Mortal Kombat" games.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:39.350
Shang Tsung of the games is said to have been
based on Lo Pan, the villain of John Carpenter's

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:41.760
beloved film, "Big Trouble in Little China."

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.210
In the 2021 "Mortal Kombat" film, however,
Shang Tsung is decked out in heavy armor that

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:50.170
does not remotely resemble the light robes
he started off wearing in the games.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:53.750
The only characteristics the "Mortal Kombat"
movie nails regarding Shang Tsung are his

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.330
shapeshifting abilities and his insatiable
thirst for human souls.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.530
"Your soul is mine!"

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:03.271
Raiden doesn't get a whole lot of time in
the ring in the new Mortal Kombat movie, unless

00:08:03.271 --> 00:08:07.430
you count the end, where he lighting-shocks
Shang Tsung back to Outworld.

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:12.360
Referred to by Liu Kang and Kung Lao only
as Lord Raiden, the god of thunder ends up

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.639
in a similar place as Shang Tsung does in
this film, taking a bit of a backseat to the

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:17.639
action.

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:20.510
Raiden is the leader and the eldest of the
Earthrealm protectors in the film.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:24.020
When it comes to the games, however, he's
a lot more important: Raiden is one of the

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:27.710
few characters who makes an appearance in
every "Mortal Kombat" game, and he's playable

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.110
in all of them, except "Mortal Kombat 3.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.979
While this god only has a small role in the
film, his silver screen design definitely

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:36.800
resembles the Raiden we see in most "Mortal
Kombat" games.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.789
He's got his defining straw hat and his unmistakable
robe ensemble — really, it's all but identical

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.370
to what he sports in recent "Mortal Kombat"
games.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.801
It would have been nice to see him flash his
moves while wearing these classic duds, but

00:08:47.801 --> 00:08:50.750
you can't have everything you want ... even
when you're a thunder god.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:56.850
A four-armed Shokan from Outworld, Goro is
one of the most instantly recognizable "Mortal

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.370
Kombat" characters ever designed.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:03.960
In the 1995 movie, Goro is portrayed by actor
Tom Woodruff, wearing a mind-bogglingly complex

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.040
costume, with actors Kevin Michael Richardson
and Frank Welker providing Goro's speaking

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.110
voice and vocal effects, respectively.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:15.730
The suit weighed roughly 125 pounds, and was
operated through a complicated combination

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.660
of puppetry, animatronics, and Woodruff's
own physicality.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:23.240
Though 1995's Goro looks dated to modern eyes,
it's hard not to be impressed by the sheer

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:26.420
volume of practical effects work that went
into creating him.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:31.540
The 2021 reboot, in contrast, takes the opposite
approach, going full-on CGI.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:35.990
This new, digitally smooth Goro gets an introduction
and a short fight scene that sees him go up

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:37.089
against Cole.

00:09:37.089 --> 00:09:41.480
Not much is explained about Goro in "Mortal
Kombat," and as a result, plenty of fun backstory

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.520
is omitted.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:46.740
In the game series' lore, Goro is the one
who killed the Great Kung Lao, ancestor to

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.430
the hat-throwing Kung Lao of the main timeline.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:54.260
This makes Goro a legendary warrior, but such
prestige doesn't come across at all in the

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.510
2021 film — instead, he feels like a henchman.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:00.740
Also noticeably absent to anyone who mains
Goro in the game series are the green fireballs

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.770
he is known to shoot from his arm and mouth.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:08.440
The single deepest cut the 2021 "Mortal Kombat"
movie makes in terms of characters has got

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.899
to be Nitara, the vampire from "Mortal Kombat:
Deadly Alliance."

00:10:11.899 --> 00:10:16.300
Introduced in the fifth main game of the series,
only ever appearing again as a playable character

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:20.620
in "Mortal Kombat: Armageddon," Nitara was
an inspired choice to include in the movie.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.400
Yet the Nitara of the silver screen is not
exactly the Nitara of the games.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:27.850
Most notably, the new movie makes a few modernizing
edits to her aesthetic.

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.420
For starters, her outfit is a lot less revealing.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:34.470
Secondly, her red bandana, which doubles as
an eye-patch for her left eye, is nowhere

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.470
to be found.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:39.870
Aside from her bat-like wings, Nitara's signature
look rests upon the bandana and the charm

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:43.170
she wears around her neck, which allows her
to warp between realms.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:47.270
Both of these defining accessories are completely
missing from the film.

